※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A lambda is written with no name, so the compiler gives its code one -- and
※ two files each writing one would give the same name twice.  What keeps them
※ apart is what keeps every other definition apart: a lambda belongs to the
※ file it is written in, and the module's name goes in front of it once every
※ route to that module is known.
※
※ So `⎕lambda1` here and `⎕lambda1` in the module beside it are two symbols,
※ and a build that linked them as one would be caught: the compiler refuses a
※ module in which two definitions come to one symbol.

let h := ⎕import("lambda-across-modules-helper")

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let g: fn(u8) → u8 = λ a: u8 → u8 { a + 2u8 }
    if g(1u8) ≠ 3u8:
        1u6
    elif h.run(1u8) ≠ 2u8:
        2u6
    else:
        0u6
